\f1\fs22 During the endless hours of Sukrit \'91Tony\'92 Supaporn\'92s monotonous labours, he made no cause to reflect on whether his was a dog\'92s life, whether the grass paid him mind, whether a bullfighter could be a lady, whether he could fall in love, whether his heart was wooden, or what colour suede shoes one could safely step on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Indeed, Tony didn\'92t think twice. Nothing was always on his mind, other than the nagging feeling that somehow someone was trying to tax him over.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jagging work in an Airborne building, in an office above the Layer, had obvious advantages. Through panoramic windows, Tony could see clear across the floating, pea-soup acid-mist to the next building, and hundreds of metres beyond. He marvelled at the swarms of hover-copts flitting between landing pads that sprouted from the sides of buildings like mushrooms on tall trees. As a child, he once saw two hover-copts fall from the clouds, their burning wrecks crashing into the neighbouring apartment block. Without forethought of danger, he and his fellow hooligans went playing among the cooling carnage. They laughed at the passengers\'92 lifeless grimaces, their lips burned away, their still-gleaming, full-toothed Colgate grins. Mirth filled the hearts of Tony and his mates with righteous comfort as they mocked the gruesomely barbecued Airborne.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A hot, smoky flash brought Tony back from his reverie and into his present-day eyes. Two hover-copts collided. As their burning, entangled carcasses broke up, they fell out of sight under the Layer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As with most of life\'92s experiences, Tony savoured this hover-copt crash with less satisfaction than his first.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

