\f0 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The Airborne never descended to the ground or streets. Instead, they sought less grimy options in the sun above the Layer, and flew in hover-copts like gluttonous insects between honey pots, greedy for social opportunities in the right restaurants with the right people. The frenzied frequency of such flitting about ensured mid-air collisions were plentiful. A fact of life, and the reasonable price of avoiding the Cloudborne. The Airborne would rather roast in a fireball than breathe the same oxygen-starved air as their unworthy, under-Layer underlings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the Layer swallowed the hover-copt wrecks, Tony dropped his mop. It slapped moistly and woodenly on the durable office floor. \'91Did you see that?\'92 he said on impulse, and received the disapproval of thin office lips and superior office eyebrows. Presently, without apparent care for the implacably deadly accident outside their windows, condescending facial features returned to business. Some remained fixed on the boorish cleaner, and gave their heads to shaking. Tony took this as an invitation to piss off somewhere else, which he did, shuffling to the main job in the office next door. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He hoped no one would report him. He needed jobs like this, and the opportunities they furnished for gazing upon the tidier, lovelier women such workplaces selected, attracted and nourished. If Tony did have thoughts, he had them of women, especially office-entitled women. He industriously catalogued them all. Finance bred large breasts. Cyber Security engendered tall blondes. In Publishing, girls with endearing lisps flourished. Biotech waifs wore lab coats and irresistible come-fuck-me eyes. Events Management produced women who could organise the end of the world, yet were incapable of cleaning their own toilets. Of all the vocations, Tony shied away only from police and People & Culture consultants. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony went a-cleaning, a deep-clean in the office of another executive who opted for the suicide retirement package (please select between gun in mouth, neck-tie of computer cable, or overdose with your choice of restricted substance). Such retirements were on the rise, and no one thought much about them. Certainly, police departments thought not much about them, and generally assigned investigations to the most junior officers they could get away with.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony resented the visceral mess of the executive\'92s resignation. Who used firearms these days? Particle-pulse weapons were quieter, more accurate, and far cleaner, fusing and sealing all wounds. No bothersome rivers of blood, just drag the body out, give the floor a quick mop, and you\'92re open again for business. But Travis Teh\'97his name was still on the door and the desk plate\'97used a truly messy and antiquated shotgun. His brains gobbed on every surface.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Tony was within finishing, a tall stunning blonde (and therefore in Cyber Security) strolled off the elevator, attracting a wake of slack-jawed gawkers of all genders from offices, meeting rooms, and water coolers. At once, her blue eyes pierced Tony to the core, and excited his heart to palpitate. Her high cheek bones, unblemished skin, and faultless teeth, drew the air from his lungs and knocked him aback. Her hair was a cascade of pure white sand, alight with random prisms of colour. Her legs, hips and breasts (insufficiently large for a role in Finance) inspired his atheist soul to desire rapturous reconciliation with God, and to bless the hapless Travis Teh who splattered himself in the service of keeping Tony busy until she arrived. With a melancholy sigh, Tony knew he would never lay his Cloudborne hands anywhere near her. A short conversation was his highest expectation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the mirror behind the office door, he saw his white overalls bearing the name 
\fs18 SUPATONES CLEANING SERVICE
\fs22 ,
\fs18  
\fs22 and the large rip in them\'97a legacy of poor tailoring and an expanding butt. The rip branded him among the most objectionable of grunts, even if he was wearing his good underwear, yielded an unflattering view of his boxers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> Tony\'92s mind dredged for something witty and original to say, something to set him apart from the crowd. Something whimsical, yet pithy. As she entered, he almost tripped over a chair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How long will you be?\'92 Her voice was joyously authoritarian to his ears, as he tried to hide the rend in his overalls. \'91I was told the office would be ready by now.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shotgun,\'92 was all he could cough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tried again, but nothing came out. Then he wheezed, and that was worse.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

