\f1\fs22 Sarah knew the effect she had on men, but she would not make this easy for him.
\f3\i  Men ain\'92t nothin\'92 but dicks and hormones, 
\f1\i0 her sister used to tell her. 
\f3\i No brains \'85 
\f1\i0 Sarah lost her train of thought, distracted by the cleaner rubbing his tush against the wall. Was he buffing away one last suicide smudge before she saw it?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you speak English?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cleaner gulped and settled. \'91Yes, English. Mr. Teh \'85 used a shotgun, ma\'92am, and \'85 well there wasn\'92t much of him to retrieve.\'92 He smiled with the achievement of completing an entire sentence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shotgun?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes ma\'92am.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you hiding something nasty?\'92 she asked the cleaner.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The question seemed to shame him. \'91Ma\'92am?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91On the wall. Step aside and let me see the wall.\'92 She flicked her hand. The cleaner jumped aside and collided with the glass office partition.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stifled a laugh as she ran her fingers over the desk and the executive chair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I would recommend replacing that,\'92 he said, sounding more relaxed. \'91Brains are a bastard to get out.\'92 He flushed with embarrassment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah held out her hand and circled an index finger like she was slowly stirring a beverage. Tony took her meaning and rotated for her. Sarah laughed when she saw the boxer shorts the rip in his overalls exposed. They were pink with little cartoon fairies. In other, more less mutant universes, the fairy would be recognised as Tinker Bell.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They were fifty percent off  in a six-pack in Floormart,\'92 Tony explained, red-faced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are those places as bad as the streams I\'92ve seen?\'92 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Floormarts? Worse. I was amazed I found a pack that was still sealed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah straightened her smile and her spine. \'91Well, next time I encounter splattered frontal lobes, I\'92ll know who to call. You should use it in advertisements.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, I see it now: 
\f3\i SupaTones Cleaning Service\'97Splattered Brains? No Stains,
\f1\i0 \'92 he quipped, surprised at the speed of his reply. \'91I have your permission to use it then, ma\'92am?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Please, I\'92m Sarah. When will you be finished here?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I just have to steam-clean the carpet. You should be fine to settle in then.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Settle in? Oh, I\'92m not working in this slaughterhouse. It smells of cleaning fluids and diarrhoea in here. And \'85\'92 Sarah sniffed the air as if for potentially toxic gases. \'91\'85 what\'92s that aftershave you\'92re wearing? 
\f3\i Is
\f1\i0  that aftershave you\'92re wearing?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s, uh, vodka,\'92 the cleaner said and scratched a particularly nasty shaving rash under his chin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f3\i Cheap
\f1\i0  vodka,\'92 Sarah said, curling her nose. \'91As for 
\f3\i my
\f1\i0  office, I\'92m at the other end of the floor. I\'92m thinking I might knock a few walls out and turn this space into a lunch room.\'92\
