\f1\fs22 Tony hoped Sarah might be a nice person, but she only made him feel ashamed. He thought, 
\f3\i Typical Airborne
\f1\i0 . With their obscene riches, they were no better than anyone else, but they acted as if their bowels made birthday cake. 
\f3\i While I\'92m taxed for life
\f1\i0 . Flourishing Cloudborne businesses rarely lived for long. Instead, they were absorbed in hostile takeovers or crushed under-foot. Every morning he woke with the fear he would be evicted from his apartment and stripped of every meagre thing he owned. \'91You know, I got the call for this gig at 4.00am. I was told to ready the office for some new computer virtuoso, and what thanks do I get? Some blonde Cyber Security clone looking down her perfect nose at me. Maybe I should squirt Breezy Brain\'92s guts back into that room.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked when she realised the cleaner came up with the same nickname for the deceased Mr Teh. Then she shrugged. \'91I\'92m sorry if you took my behaviour as rude, Mr Fairy Boxers. Will there be anything else?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony set his jaw and inflated his chest. \'91You\'92ve been marked for bigger things, eh?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who told you that?\'92 she blurted out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ma\'92am. I\'92m not at liberty to discuss it,\'92 he said, swelling with the point he\'92d scored.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah leaned forward enough for her blouse to gape and present a seductively safe glimpse of her 
\f3\i lingerie d\'92mamaries. 
\f1\i0 \'91Tell me how a cleaner would know anything about me.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With the pleasure of his brief win souring, Tony swallowed and froze. Sarah stared and waited him out with an excoriating stare. \'91Okay, okay,\'92 he said to kick-start his lungs. The remainder came in a single blurt. \'91I have a friend\'97well, more of an acquaintance. Actually, not so much an acquaintance as \'85 never mind. He\'92s in Public Relations\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which makes him a liar.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'97of the Department of Traffic Movement and Public Security\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, he\'92s a TRAMP! A colossal liar then.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92s in Homicide.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A colossal homicidal liar.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I think calling him homicidal is a bit purple.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is he in Homicide or Public Relations?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony puzzled about that. \'91He\'92s host of 
\f3\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged and shook her head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The public broadcast show? Anyway, I think the TRAMPS Commissioner sends him in to close high-profile cases fast. And whenever there are late night incidents of a people-dying-horribly nature, he directs the cleaning work my way. That\'92s it, I swear. He heard the new exec was a high-flyer, that\'92s all.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s eyes narrowed. \'91Do you scrub evidence as poorly as you scrub away gore?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No? \'85 No! Never,\'92 Tony said, trying to keep his tells from telling. \'91I never mess with any evidence. I\'92m never late. I get the job done quickly, so everyone can keep up with the making money.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, and I should be on with it. Good day, Mr Fairy Boxers.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A deflated Tony left. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah checked her door was opaqued and locked, then raised her arms triumphantly above her head. She was on her way up, however this gig evolved.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

